  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 4♥ South starts with 9 top tricks (the ♠A, 7 heart tricks, and the ♦A). The ♦Q may be worth a trick, via a finesse, if West has the ♦K. The ♣K may also be South's tenth trick if West has the ♣A.\n\nAfter winning the opening lead and drawing trump, South should play on clubs. If South makes the mistake of taking the diamond finesse and East has the ♦K, East will win the trick and return a diamond. The defense will have the setting trick in diamonds established before South has a chance to try clubs.\n\nIf South plays his ♣2 after drawing trump, he will succeed immediately if West has the ♣A. If it turns out that the ♣A is with East, South will still have time to try the diamond finesse. South must be careful not to duck the opening ♠K lead. A diamond lead through North would not be welcome.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠K against 4♥ South starts with 9 top tricks (the ♠A, 7 heart tricks, and the ♦A). The ♦Q may be worth a trick, via a finesse, if West has the ♦K. The ♣K may also be South's tenth trick if West has the ♣A.",
    "After winning the opening lead and drawing trump, South should play on clubs. If South makes the mistake of taking the diamond finesse and East has the ♦K, East will win the trick and return a diamond. The defense will have the setting trick in diamonds established before South has a chance to try clubs.",
    "If South plays his ♣2 after drawing trump, he will succeed immediately if West has the ♣A. If it turns out that the ♣A is with East, South will still have time to try the diamond finesse. South must be careful not to duck the opening ♠K lead. A diamond lead through North would not be welcome."
